Analyses: NameResolver and Scope
********************************

.[perex]
Two built-in analyses answer two questions every code tool asks: what a given name really means, and whether `$this` is available at a given place in the code.


What an Analysis Is
===================

The tree tells you what the code looks like. An analysis is information derived from the whole file that the tree itself does not carry: what the name `Order` means in this file, or whether `$this` is available. It is an ordinary class built on top of `FileNode`; you do not register anything or implement any interface.

```php
$resolver = new PhpSyntax\Analyses\NameResolver($file);
$scope = new PhpSyntax\Analyses\Scope;
```

An analysis that remembers derived information reads the tree as it was when the analysis was created; after the tree changes, create the analysis again. You can tell that the tree has changed from `FileNode::$revision`. This applies to `NameResolver`. `Scope` remembers nothing and does not even take the file: it finds the answer by walking up the parents of the node it gets, so no change can make it stale.


NameResolver
============

`NameResolver` resolves names using the namespace and the imports, including what PHP does at runtime: an unqualified function or constant that does not exist in the namespace falls back to the global one.

Imports apply in the block where they are written, while declarations belong to the whole namespace. So a second `namespace A { }` block in the same file sees a function declared by the first block, but not what the first block imported. On its own, the resolver knows nothing about symbols the file does not declare: the fallback to the global namespace is then a guess based on what is visible in the file, not knowledge of the symbol. The guess becomes knowledge if you pass a `NamespacedSymbols` object to the resolver's constructor: a list of functions and constants that namespaces declare outside the file, and a `complete` flag saying there are no others. The lists may also be empty, and that is exactly what a project with no functions or constants in namespaces looks like. `getUnqualifiedResolution()` tells you whether a particular answer rests only on a guess.

```php
$symbols = new PhpSyntax\Analyses\NamespacedSymbols(
	functions: ['App\Utils\format'],
	constants: ['App\VERSION'],
	complete: true,
);
$resolver = new PhpSyntax\Analyses\NameResolver($file, $symbols);
$symbols->hasFunction('App\Utils\format');   // true
$symbols->hasConstant('app\VERSION');        // true: namespace regardless of case, constant name exactly
```

Names in `NamespacedSymbols` must be the full names of symbols in a namespace; the constructor rejects a bare `format` with an `InvalidArgumentException`, because global functions do not belong in the list.

```php
$resolver->getNamespace($node);         // 'App\Model', '' in the global namespace
$resolver->getImports(PhpSyntax\SymbolKind::ClassLike, $node);   // alias => full name, valid at the place of the node
$resolver->getImports(PhpSyntax\SymbolKind::Function, $node);
$resolver->getImports(PhpSyntax\SymbolKind::Constant, $node);

$resolver->resolve($name);              // 'App\Model\Order' from a NameNode, as a class, function or constant, depending on where the name stands
$resolver->resolveClass($name);         // the same with the kind of symbol chosen in advance
$resolver->resolveFunction($name);
$resolver->resolveConstant($name);

$resolver->isGlobalFunctionCall($node, 'sizeof');            // a call of a global function, or of that particular one
$resolver->findGlobalFunction($call, ['count', 'sizeof']);   // which of these global functions the call calls, or null
$resolver->getUnqualifiedResolution($call->name);            // Global, Namespaced, or Uncertain when the global fallback is only a guess
$resolver->getUnqualifiedResolution('count', PhpSyntax\SymbolKind::Function, $node); // the same for a name given as text
```

The keys of the imports array are the aliases in the form PHP compares them: in lowercase for classes and functions, where case does not matter (`'strings' => 'Nette\Utils\Strings'`), and as written for constants. The answer of `getUnqualifiedResolution()` is the enum `PhpSyntax\UnqualifiedResolution`. A name given as a `NameNode` carries both the kind of symbol and the place where it stands; a name given as text needs both as two more arguments.

`resolve()` chooses between `resolveClass()`, `resolveFunction()` and `resolveConstant()` by the `$symbolKind` property of the name, that is, by where the name stands; a detached name is read as a class. All four need a name that really refers to some symbol, and not every `NameNode` does: `int` in a type names a built-in type, `Foo` in `use Foo;` only introduces a symbol, and `self` stands for a class only where it is written. For such a name, the resolver throws an `InvalidArgumentException` instead of silently reading it as a class of the namespace and returning nonsense like `App\int`. So if you go through all the names in a file, ask [isReference() |tree#Names] first:

```php
foreach ($file->find(NameNode::class) as $name) {
	if ($name->isReference()) {
		echo $resolver->resolve($name);
	}
}
```

There is one exception: `resolveClass()` does not fail on `self`, `static` and `parent`, but returns them as written.

`isGlobalFunctionCall()` answers a question every tool used to answer on its own, usually worse: the node must be a call of a function by name (not `$f()` or `$obj->f()`), the name must not be a keyword, and after resolving, it must be global. This is how you know that `count($a)` inside `namespace App` calls `count` and not `App\count`, unless such a function is imported, declared in the file or listed in `NamespacedSymbols`. Without a complete `NamespacedSymbols`, however, it is a guess: `App\count()` may be declared by another file of the project that the resolver does not see, and PHP would then call that one. When you care about several functions at once, `findGlobalFunction()` returns the one the call calls, written as in the list, regardless of the case used in the code; it counts a call as global the same way `isGlobalFunctionCall()` does.

A tool that changes code based on the answer should therefore also ask `getUnqualifiedResolution()`. This is how [DressCode |dresscode:namespaces] decides whether a fix is risky: with the answer `Uncertain` it is, with `Global` or `Namespaced` it is not. The method accepts only an unqualified name, because a name with a backslash never falls back to the global namespace, and for any other name it throws an `InvalidArgumentException`. The answer `Global` says that the name points to the global namespace, not necessarily to the symbol of that name: an import with an alias can redirect it elsewhere.

A name you have just parsed, which is not in the tree yet, does not know where it will be; the second argument tells where you want it resolved:

```php
$name = $builder->name('Strings');
$resolver->resolveClass($name, $at);    // resolved as it would be at the place of $at
```

The resolver rejects a node from another tree with an exception, instead of answering as if the node were in the global namespace. A quiet, valid-looking answer to a question that was never properly asked is worse than an error.

The opposite direction is just as useful: how to **write** a full name at the place where a node stands.

```php
$kind = PhpSyntax\SymbolKind::ClassLike;

$resolver->shortenName('Nette\Utils\Strings', $kind, $at);   // 'Strings', it is imported
$resolver->shortenName('App\Model\Order', $kind, $at);       // 'Order' in namespace App\Model
$resolver->shortenName('Other\Thing', $kind, $at);           // '\Other\Thing', otherwise it would be looked up in the namespace
$resolver->isAliasFree('Strings', $kind, $at);                // false, that alias already means something
```

`shortenName()` takes into account aliases, prefix imports and the namespace, and returns the shortest form that means the same thing at that place. "Means the same thing" is a strict condition: where the name is taken by an import, it returns a longer form instead. So with `use function X\count;` in the file, the global `count` gets `'\count'`, because a bare `count` would mean `X\count` at that place. For the same reason, a global function or constant inside a namespace gets a bare name only when the resolver got `NamespacedSymbols` with the `complete` flag; without that knowledge, a bare `count` could mean `App\count` from another file. So when you read the result back with `resolveFunction()` and the others, you always get back to the symbol you asked about. `isAliasFree()` answers the question anyone who wants to add an import asks: is this name still free here?

And three more methods, this time about the declarations in the file:

```php
$resolver->getDeclaredName($class);              // 'App\Cart' from a declaration node
$resolver->findDeclaration('App\Cart', $kind);   // ClassLikeNode, or null
$resolver->findNamespacedDeclarations($file);    // functions and constants the node declares into a namespace
```

`findDeclaration()` searches among symbols of one kind, because PHP keeps a class, a function and a constant of the same name apart: `SymbolKind::ClassLike` finds a class, interface, trait or enum, `Function` a function, and `Constant` an item of a `const` statement. It treats case the way PHP does, so for a constant it is case-sensitive only in the constant's own name, and a leading backslash does not matter. Declarations nested in a condition or in a function body count too, and when the file declares the same name more than once, for example under `if (!function_exists(...))`, you get the first one. `getDeclaredName()` returns the name even for such a constant; a class constant introduces nothing into a namespace, so it gets `null`.

`findNamespacedDeclarations()` returns the functions and constants that a node declares into a namespace, as a list of `Declaration` objects with the kind of symbol (`$kind`), the full name (`$name`) and the node that names it (`$node`), in the order they are written. It also counts declarations in a condition or in a function body, and constants named by a string in `define()`; what is declared into the global namespace is not among them. It is exactly what belongs in `NamespacedSymbols`: run it over the files of the project and build from the results the complete list for the resolver of each file.


Scope
=====

`Scope` tells you whether `$this` is available at the place of a node or a token:

```php
$scope->hasThis($node);   // in a non-static method or hook, and in a closure inside them
```

`hasThis()` also takes into account that a closure and an arrow function take `$this` from their surroundings by themselves, without `use`, while `static function` and `static fn` do not. A property hook always has it, because it belongs to the object.

Finding the function and the class that contain the code needs no analysis; going up the parents is enough:

```php
$node->findAncestor(FunctionLikeNode::class);   // function, method, closure, arrow function or hook, or null
$node->findAncestor(ClassLikeNode::class);      // class, interface, trait, enum or anonymous class, or null
$closure->getCapturedVariableNames();           // variables from use (...), without the dollar: ['a', 'b']
```

The same works from a token too, see [Up and Sideways |traversal#Up and Sideways].


Your Own Analysis
=================

When several places need the same information about a file, write it as a class whose constructor takes a `FileNode`. A tool built on PhpSyntax usually creates such an analysis itself and throws it away when the revision of the tree changes; [DressCode |dresscode:rule-contract#Analyses] does this for its rules, and besides the two built-in analyses it adds `PhpDoc` for doc comments, because that one needs a phpDoc parser, which does not belong in PhpSyntax.
